  "mechanismSummary": "Triethylamine removes the acidic C–H of the thiazolium salt (the catalyst used by Stetter, 3-benzyl-5-(2-hydroxyethyl)-4-methylthiazolium chloride, a model of thiamine). The ylide, an N-heterocyclic carbene, adds to benzaldehyde. After two proton transfers the adduct becomes the Breslow intermediate, an enaminol whose former carbonyl carbon is nucleophilic: umpolung. It attacks a second benzaldehyde, the new alkoxide is protonated, and the OH next to the ring gives up its proton as the C=O forms and the ylide leaves. The ylide is protonated again, and the cycle is closed. Breslow proposed this mechanism in 1958 for thiamine, nature's carbene catalyst; cyanide does the same job by the older Lapworth mechanism. Every step is reversible.",
